# spray-json's recursive-descent JSON parser recursed into nested objects
# and arrays with no depth limit, so a deeply nested JSON document threw a
# StackOverflowError and took the parsing thread down (denial of service
# through unbounded recursion, CWE-674/CWE-400; PR #284, fixes #286).
# `value` in src/main/scala/spray/json/JsonParser.scala dispatched on the
# cursor character to `object`/`array`, whose member and element loops
# called `value` again -- the nesting depth bounded only by the input's own
# nesting, the stack the only budget. The fixing commit d56d7f42134 (the
# merge "fix-uncontrolled-recursion"; the commit inside it, a558753, names
# the CVE in its own message) adds a JsonParserSettings.maxDepth defaulting
# to 1000, threads a remainingNesting parameter through
# `value`/`object`/`array`, and throws a ParsingException once the remaining
# nesting reaches zero.
#
# ATTENTION, rechecked. The depth-budget family of 1559/1586/1612/1697:
# the corpus's CWE-400 rules judge per-iteration progress, not a depth
# carried through recursion, so nothing can ask whether the parse stops at
# a depth cap. Re-measured on the real checkout, three --cache off runs per
# side, stable: the full tree (28 scala files) reports 0 findings and 0
# flags at both revisions, and `vyql match` on JsonParser.scala labels
# nothing at all -- no source, no sink, no control -- so no rule was firing
# that a flags-only view hid. The later-shipped nesting-ceiling vocabulary
# does not reach here: NestedExtglobExpansionWithoutDepthLimit is bound in
# javascript and NestedParamExpansionWithoutDepthLimit in ruby, and both
# concepts state a query-string or glob convention rather than structured
# text parsing, so neither a Scala emitter nor reuse of the concept fits.
#
# What a Scala binding can key on is the complete inventory the frontend
# emits for a function context (scFunctionContext, scala.go:291): the
# function's name and the assign/call_path/call/call_arg/selector/expr/
# literal tokens of its body. There is no module or class context node, no
# match-construct token, and no fact for a call resolving to an enclosing
# function, so the mutual cycle value -> object -> value is not addressable.
# Measured with probe bindings on the real code:
#
#   * The discriminator is available. A function-scope binding whose
#     positives hold at `value` and whose negation is the generic ceiling
#     spellings already shipped with the ruby binding (max_depth, maxDepth,
#     max_nesting, maxNesting, ...) reports 1 at the fix parent and 0 at the
#     fix. The ceiling names land in a literal token through the patched
#     exception's interpolated string, and a Scala function context carries
#     only AST-derived tokens, so a comment cannot silence the negation.
#   * The positives are not. Requiring both container-open delimiters as
#     substring matches fires 34 times across 25 ordinary scala files for
#     one true positive. Requiring them as exact literal tokens narrows
#     that to one file -- and that file is a value formatter, not a parser:
#     zeppelin's CqlFormatter.formatHuman delimits its output with
#     mkString("{", ", ", "}") and mkString("[", ", ", "]"), so the
#     conjuncts select code that handles both container kinds, not code
#     that parses. The reachable set is exhaustive: all 774 non-target
#     scala files under the corpus clones, scanned in five bounded chunks.
#   * The exact-token form also misses the sibling instance of this same
#     weakness in this same language: play's FormUtils.doFromJson walks a
#     JsValue with no depth bound and dispatches on JsObject and JsArray
#     types, not on characters, so it carries no delimiter literal at all.
#     A shape that catches spray-json's character-level spelling while
#     missing the type-level spelling of the same unbounded walk keys on
#     this library's implementation style rather than the weakness, and the
#     next variant walks past it. Keying on spray-json's own identifiers
#     instead detects the library, and minting a concept for the class adds
#     a fifth per-shape sibling to a weakness class that already carries
#     four.
#
# Not an engine gap: extraction and resolution work (`vyql graph` carries
# JsonParser.`value` and the Call path=`value` re-entry), and the missing
# piece is a recursion-depth observation over a call-graph cycle plus the
# language-neutral rule over it -- design work the self-recursion entry
# already records, and no analysis.recursion.* fact exists in the frontend
# today. No Scala corpus exists in either benchmark suite, so a broad scala
# shape has no precision gate to be measured against at all.
#
# The spec pins the miss against the parser-progress rule of the right
# class. When depth-budget vocabulary lands, the unbounded parser and the
# type-dispatched sibling walk must flip to expect; the depth-capped parser
# must stay rejected, and the formatter's flag is the recorded cost of any
# delimiter-keyed positives.

test "rank1776: the json parser recurses without a depth cap"
  lang scala
  file src/main/scala/spray/json/JsonParser.scala
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  code
  ```
  package spray.json

  class JsonParser(input: ParserInput) {
    private var jsValue: JsValue = _

    def parseJsValue(): JsValue = {
      `value`()
      jsValue
    }

    private def `value`(): Unit = {
      (cursorChar: @switch) match {
        case 'f' => `false`()
        case 'n' => `null`()
        case 't' => `true`()
        case '{' => advance(); `object`()
        case '[' => advance(); `array`()
        case '"' => `string`()
        case _ => fail("JSON Value")
      }
    }

    private def `object`(): Unit = {
      if (cursorChar != '}') {
        @tailrec def members(): Unit = {
          `string`()
          require(':')
          `value`()
          if (ws(',')) members()
        }
        members()
      }
    }

    private def `array`(): Unit = {
      if (cursorChar != ']') {
        @tailrec def values(): Unit = {
          `value`()
          if (ws(',')) values()
        }
        values()
      }
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1776: the fixed revision threads a nesting budget through the recursion"
  lang scala
  file src/main/scala/spray/json/JsonParser.scala
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  code
  ```
  package spray.json

  class JsonParser(input: ParserInput, settings: JsonParserSettings) {
    private var jsValue: JsValue = _

    def parseJsValue(): JsValue = {
      `value`(settings.maxDepth)
      jsValue
    }

    private def `value`(remainingNesting: Int): Unit =
      if (remainingNesting == 0)
        throw new ParsingException(
          "JSON input nested too deeply",
          s"JSON input was nested more deeply than the configured limit of maxNesting = ${settings.maxDepth}")
      else {
        (cursorChar: @switch) match {
          case 'f' => `false`()
          case 'n' => `null`()
          case 't' => `true`()
          case '{' => advance(); `object`(remainingNesting)
          case '[' => advance(); `array`(remainingNesting)
          case '"' => `string`()
          case _ => fail("JSON Value")
        }
      }

    private def `object`(remainingNesting: Int): Unit = {
      if (cursorChar != '}') {
        @tailrec def members(): Unit = {
          `string`()
          require(':')
          `value`(remainingNesting - 1)
          if (ws(',')) members()
        }
        members()
      }
    }

    private def `array`(remainingNesting: Int): Unit = {
      if (cursorChar != ']') {
        @tailrec def values(): Unit = {
          `value`(remainingNesting - 1)
          if (ws(',')) values()
        }
        values()
      }
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1776: the same unbounded walk dispatched on value types carries no delimiter literal"
  lang scala
  file core/play/src/main/scala/play/api/data/Form.scala
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  code
  ```
  package play.api.data

  import play.api.libs.json._

  private[data] object FormUtils {
    def fromJson(js: JsValue, maxChars: Long): Map[String, String] =
      doFromJson(FromJsonRoot(js), Map.empty, 0, maxChars)

    @annotation.tailrec
    private def doFromJson(
        context: FromJsonContext,
        form: Map[String, String],
        cumulativeChars: Int,
        maxChars: Long
    ): Map[String, String] = context match {
      case FromJsonTerm              => form
      case ctx: FromJsonContextValue =>
        ctx.next
        ctx.value match {
          case obj: JsObject if obj.fields.nonEmpty =>
            doFromJson(FromJsonObject(ctx, obj.fields.toIndexedSeq, 0), form, cumulativeChars, maxChars)
          case JsArray(values) if values.nonEmpty =>
            doFromJson(FromJsonArray(ctx, values.toIndexedSeq, 0), form, cumulativeChars, maxChars)
          case JsNull | JsArray(_) | JsObject(_) =>
            doFromJson(ctx.next, form, cumulativeChars, maxChars)
          case simple =>
            val value = simple match {
              case JsString(v) => v
              case JsNumber(v) => v.toString
            }
            val prefix             = ctx.prefix
            val newCumulativeChars = cumulativeChars + prefix.length + value.length
            if (newCumulativeChars > maxChars) {
              throw FormJsonExpansionTooLarge(maxChars)
            }
            doFromJson(ctx.next, form.updated(prefix, value), newCumulativeChars, maxChars)
        }
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1776: a value formatter delimits both container kinds without parsing anything"
  lang scala
  file cassandra/src/main/scala/org/apache/zeppelin/cassandra/CqlFormatter.scala
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  code
  ```
  package org.apache.zeppelin.cassandra

  class CqlFormatter {
    def formatHuman(obj: Object): String = {
      if (obj == null) {
        "null"
      } else {
        obj match {
          case m: java.util.Map[Object, Object] =>
            m.asScala.map { case (k, v) => formatHuman(k) + ": " + formatHuman(v) }.mkString("{", ", ", "}")
          case l: java.util.List[Object] =>
            l.asScala.map(x => formatHuman(x)).mkString("[", ", ", "]")
          case s: java.util.Set[Object] =>
            s.asScala.map(x => formatHuman(x)).mkString("{", ", ", "}")
          case t: TupleValue =>
            (0 until t.size()).map(i => formatHuman(t.getObject(i))).mkString("(", ", ", ")")
          case other =>
            other.toString
        }
      }
    }
  }
  ```
